      "id": "phy_work_and_energy:rc-1",
      "chapter_id": "phy_work_and_energy",
      "section_id": "sec-2",
      "stem": {
        "text": "A force of 10 N acts at 60 degrees to the direction of the motion, over a distance of 4 m. How much work is done?",
        "inline": []
      },
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "40 J"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "20 J"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "10 J"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "5 J"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "b",
      "explanation": {
        "text": "Work is force times the displacement along the force, so W = 10 x 4 x cos 60 = 20 J.",
        "inline": []
      },
      "difficulty": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "phy_work_and_energy:rc-5",
      "chapter_id": "phy_work_and_energy",
      "section_id": "sec-7",
      "stem": {
        "text": "A block slides down a rough slope and its mechanical energy falls. What has happened to the energy?",
        "inline": []
      },
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "It has been destroyed"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "It has become thermal energy"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "It has become kinetic energy"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "It is unchanged"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "b",
      "explanation": {
        "text": "Total energy is conserved. Friction turns the mechanical energy into thermal energy, so the mechanical energy falls by exactly that amount.",
        "inline": []
      },
      "difficulty": 2
    },
    {
